../formatting/screenplay-format-spec.md for the underlying industry format rules.

Target length: 90-120 pages (1 page ~= 1 minute of screen time).
This skeleton is pre-loaded with Save the Cat's 15 beats as section markers (##) and
synopsis lines (=) so you can outline in this same file before you write a word of scene.
Delete the section/synopsis scaffolding once you're drafting for real, or keep it —
Fountain sections are non-printing, so they never show up in the exported script.
*/

# ACT ONE

## 1. Opening Image (p.1)
= The "before" snapshot. Establish tone and the hero's starting state.

INT./EXT. << LOCATION >> - << DAY/NIGHT >>

<< Action: introduce the world and the protagonist in their status quo. >>

## 2. Theme Stated (p.5)
= Someone (often not the hero) states what the story is really about.

<< CHARACTER >>
<< A line of dialogue that plants the theme. >>

## 3. Set-Up (p.1-10)
= Hero, flaws, the things that need fixing, the A-cast.

## 4. Catalyst (p.12)
= The inciting incident. The event that breaks the status quo.

## 5. Debate (p.12-25)
= The hero hesitates: should I do this?

## 6. Break Into Two (p.25)
= The hero chooses. Enters the new world.

# ACT TWO

## 7. B Story (p.30)
= A secondary relationship that will carry the theme.

## 8. Fun and Games (p.30-55)
= The "promise of the premise" delivered.

## 9. Midpoint (p.55)
= A false victory or false defeat. Stakes raise.

## 10. Bad Guys Close In (p.55-75)
= External pressure mounts; internal doubts regroup.

## 11. All Is Lost (p.75)
= The lowest point. A "whiff of death."

## 12. Dark Night of the Soul (p.75-85)
= Hopelessness, before the answer is found.

# ACT THREE

## 13. Break Into Three (p.85)
= The solution appears. A and B stories fuse.

## 14. Finale (p.85-110)
= Execute the plan. Prove the change. Build the new world.

## 15. Final Image (p.110)
= The "after" snapshot. Mirror-opposite of the Opening Image.

FADE OUT.

> THE END <
